+# Fixed BT.709 limited-range compatibility conversion
+
+`common/ColorConversion.cpp` ports the arithmetic and AArch64 NEON implementation
+from the reviewed native Q6A `helper_709.c`. `VideoPixelFormat::ABGR` input is
+interpreted as interleaved **R, G, B, A bytes**. Alpha is ignored. RGB input uses
+this fixed matrix; the existing YUV copy/conversion branches retain their data.
+This change does not add general matrix, transfer or range negotiation.
+
+The scalar and vector coefficients, 2x2 chroma averaging and edge duplication
+are unchanged. The source port adds stride, address-overflow and disjoint-plane
+footprint checks before any output write. Raw pointers do not encode allocation
+sizes: callers must provide mapped spans covering the documented rows.
+`FormatConverter` checks its visible block geometry, preserves the type-0
+IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED RGB mapping fallback and rejects unsupported RGB layouts.
+
+Run from any directory on a host with C and C++ compilers:
+
+```sh
+sh tests/host/run_color_conversion.sh
+```
+
+The test compiles the retained reference helper and the new implementation,
+then compares all visible and padding bytes across 3,490 cases: known colors,
+RGBA alpha independence, U/V order, random input, odd utility edges, vector
+boundary widths, padding, signed row strides, source flips, 4K geometry and
+invalid-input output canaries. AddressSanitizer and UBSan are enabled.
+LeakSanitizer is disabled by the test because some execution sandboxes cannot
+read proc task lists. This is a host contract test, not an Android build or a
+new AArch64 performance measurement. The AArch64 vector operations are retained
+from the previously tested helper; the new source build still needs target
+compilation and the native H.264/HEVC Surface regression.
